opening = md`After every deploy the deployer runs the deployed module's tests itself, alone, in a browser of the cluster, and acts on the result. A Worker that is not verified in 10 minutes is put back, and its next deploy is refused until then. For the owner and for every agent that deploys: a deploy is kept or put back by what was deployed, and not by which tab happened to be open.`
// %%
viewof specStatus = sticky(Inputs.select(["draft", "in review", "approved for handover"], { label: "Status", value: "draft" }), "draft")
// %%
readiness = {
  const notes = (annotations || []).filter((a) => a.home === specModule || (a.anchor && a.anchor.module === specModule));
  const unchecked = requirements.filter((r) => !r.check || /^<.*>$/.test(r.check.trim()));
  const blocking = questions.filter((q) => q.blocking && !q.answer);
  const undrawn = states.filter((s) => !s.drawing);
  const items = [
    [notes.length, "annotations to resolve"],
    [blocking.length, "blocking questions open"],
    [unchecked.length, `of ${requirements.length} requirements without a check`],
    [undrawn.length, `of ${states.length} states without a drawing`]
  ];
  const ready = items.every(([n]) => n === 0) && specStatus === "approved for handover";
  // Everything that still needs the reviewer, listed here with a link to where it is answered.
  const open = questions.map((q, i) => ({ q, i })).filter(({ q }) => !q.answer);
  const todo = [
    ...open.filter(({ q }) => q.blocking).map(({ q, i }) => ({ tag: "blocking question", text: q.q, to: `[data-question="${i}"]` })),
    ...open.filter(({ q }) => !q.blocking).map(({ q, i }) => ({ tag: "open question", text: q.q, to: `[data-question="${i}"]` })),
    ...unchecked.map((r) => ({ tag: "no check", text: `${r.id} ${r.text}`, to: `[data-requirement="${r.id}"]` })),
    ...undrawn.map((s) => ({ tag: "not drawn", text: `state: ${s.state}`, to: `[data-state="${s.state}"]` }))
  ];
  const box = htl.html`<div style="font:13px system-ui;padding:.5em .8em;border:1px solid color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 25%, transparent);border-radius:6px"></div>`;
  // Scrolls the pane; a plain #fragment link would replace the lopepage layout hash.
  const jump = (to) => (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const el = (box.closest(".lp2-pane") || box.ownerDocument).querySelector(to);
    if (!el) return;
    el.scrollIntoView({ block: "center", behavior: "smooth" });
    if (el.animate) el.animate([{ background: "color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 22%, transparent)" }, { background: "transparent" }], 1800);
  };
  const short = (s) => (s.length > 110 ? s.slice(0, 110) + "…" : s);
  box.append(htl.html`<div style="display:flex;gap:1.2em;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:baseline">
    <strong>${ready ? "Ready to hand over" : "Not ready to hand over"}</strong>
    ${items.map(([n, label]) => htl.html`<span style=${{ opacity: n ? 1 : 0.5 }}><strong>${n}</strong> ${label}</span>`)}
  </div>`);
  if (todo.length) box.append(htl.html`<ol style="margin:.5em 0 0;padding-left:1.4em">${todo.map((t) => htl.html`<li><a href="#" onclick=${jump(t.to)}>${t.tag}</a>: ${short(t.text)}</li>`)}</ol>`);
  return box;
}
// %%
md`## Reviewing this spec

- **Leave a note**: ☰ → Annotate → Note, then drag over the words or click the shape. Notes are review comments; Claude Code works through them, and you delete each when it is dealt with.
- **Ask about a spot**: ☰ → Annotate → Ask, then pick the words or shape and type. The question goes to the agent with what you pointed at, and the answer threads at that spot. It reads Ask Claude while a Claude Code session is paired and Ask robocoop otherwise; the switchboard tab changes which.
- **Click prose** to rewrite it. This spec has no drawings.
- **Ask** in the pane at the bottom. Roboco-op reads and edits this notebook.
- ☰ → **Save in place** keeps your edits and notes in this file.`
// %%
md`## Problem

On 2026-10-10 at 19:32:38 CEST the deployer put \`brain-x-issues\` back to its previous version and a reviewed fix was off cb4 for 11 minutes. Nothing was wrong with the fix. An agent had opened the Brain's page in the cluster's browser with the owner's session. That browser is Chrome 128, the tracker then signed with Ed25519, Chrome 128 has none, the page reported the tracker's tests as failed, and the deployer acted on the report.

What the code does today (read 2026-10-10):

- The deployer checks health only. \`/xrpc/_health\` twice failed puts a Worker back (\`brain-deployer.ojs\`, the tick at line 796).
- The tests are run by the owner's page. \`assembled\` (\`cloud-brain.ojs:826\`) runs \`verifyService\` for every Worker when the page opens with the owner signed in, and \`verifyService\` (\`cloud-brain.ojs:790\`) sends \`deploy.report\` to the core (\`brain-core.ojs:524\`).
- One failed report on a hash not yet verified has the deployer put the version before back (\`verdictOf\`, \`brain-deployer.ojs:850\`).
- \`brain.ts apply\` reports nothing, so a Worker deployed from the command line stays unverified until the owner opens the page. Five versions of the tracker were deployed that way on 2026-10-10, each over an unverified one.
- Only the kernel, the core and the Worker that serves the page are put back when nobody confirms in 10 minutes (\`brain-deployer.ojs:525\`). Every other Worker stays unverified with no limit.

So a verdict depends on whose browser opens the page, which browser it is, and what else that page is doing. Measured the same day at 22:11 in the cluster's Chrome 128: the published notebook opened with only the tracker in view ran every module's tests at once (the build boots \`@tomlarkworthy/tests\` and 42 mains), and 18 of the tracker's 19 tests had no result in 60 s. Heap 640 MB.`
// %%
md`## Outcome

- A deploy of a Worker is followed, with no person and no page involved, by a run of that Worker's \`test_\` cells alone, and the deployer's log has \`deploy.verified\`, or \`deploy.failed\` and \`deploy.rollback\`, with the hash and the failed tests.
- A Worker deployed and not verified within 10 minutes is put back to the version before. This holds for every Worker deployed with \`infra.apply\`. A member's Worker (\`member.deploy\`) is not changed.
- A second deploy of a Worker that is still unverified is refused with a message that says when it will be decided. A deploy of any other Worker goes on.
- Opening the Brain's page, in any browser, signed in or not, changes no Worker.

Tom, 2026-10-10: "the report definately needs the hash of what it is verifying so the wrong report doesn;t rollback the wrong worker. It should not be mandatory because it might be lost. But if something has proof the deployment is broken then it is correct to roll it back. We do not want poisoned code infecting the cluster and comounding. But the deployer should have an isolated version testing what is deployed does what is expected." And: "you can't do another deployment until verified ... the 10 min rollback is mandatory liveliness feature. We can't have the next thing building on a faulty base. So its mega important the verification occur quickly and on an isolated code base."`
// %%
md`### Size

Three days. The deployer's state machine and the refusal are one day with tests. The verify page and the run are one day. The page's change, the docs, and the first deploys on cb4 with the owner are the third. If time runs out the rows at the bottom of Behaviour go first.`
// %%
md`## Scope

### Not your concern, do not build it

- \`cloud-brain-dev\`, the repository kept by \`brain-x-git\`, with \`sync.ts\` and \`test.ts\` (tests under bun with no browser). Another session owns it. Its \`services.json\` is not read by this design.
- \`brain-x-container\`. Not used.
- The tracker (\`brain-issues.ojs\`), its signing, its panel.
- The approval of a deploy by the owner (\`approval\` in the deployer's settings, \`pending/\`). Unchanged: verification starts when a Worker is deployed, which is after any approval.
- \`brain.ts page up\` and the pairing bridge.
- The health check and its two-fails rule. Kept as it is.

### Non-goals

- Running the tests before the deploy. What is tested is what runs, read back from the Worker; a test before the deploy tests what was sent.
- A second browser or a newer Chrome. Cloudflare's Browser Run is Chrome 128.0.6613.137 and documents no way to choose (read 2026-10-10; \`cloudflare/playwright\` issue 232). The design does not depend on the version: a module whose tests need a newer browser fails its verification and is put back, which is the correct answer for a Brain whose only browser is that one.
- Verifying a member's Worker (\`member.deploy\`). Members' modules have no tests the owner vouches for.
- Making the page smaller than the module's imports. The tracker's page is 4.9 MB of 54 modules, and ran in 3.8 s.`
// %%
md`## Words

The implementer uses these strings as written.`
// %%
copy_title = md`Deploy verification`
// %%
copy_opening = md`The deployer runs a Worker's tests after each deploy, alone, in a browser of the Brain. Until they pass the Worker is on probation: it is put back after 10 minutes, and it cannot be deployed again.`
// %%
copy_strings = md`| where | text |
|---|---|
| a deploy refused while the Worker is unverified (\`reason\` of the result, state \`refused\`) | \`NAME is HASH12 and not verified yet: its tests are running, and it is put back at HH:MM:SS if they have not passed. Deploy again after that.\` |
| put back, tests failed (\`deploy.rollback\` reason) | \`its tests failed: test_a, test_b: MESSAGE\` (as today, 200 characters) |
| put back, no verdict in time | \`not confirmed in 10 minutes\` (as today, \`nextKernelState\`) |
| log line, a verdict for a hash that is not running | \`{ at: "deploy.verify.stale", target, hash, running }\` |
| log line, run started | \`{ at: "deploy.verify", target, hash, run }\` |
| log line, verdict | \`{ at: "deploy.verified", target, hash, tests, otherTestsRan, ms }\` or \`{ at: "deploy.failed", target, hash, failed, otherTestsRan, ms }\` |
| log line, the run gave no verdict | \`{ at: "deploy.verify.none", target, hash, reason }\` |
| deployer page, a Worker on probation | \`NAME HASH12 is on probation until HH:MM:SS.\` (as today, now for every Worker) |`
// %%
md`## Behaviour

One requirement per row, in priority order: if time runs out, the bottom rows go first. \`check\` is how anyone can tell the row holds. The \`test_\` names are cells to be written in the module named.`
// %%
requirements = [
  { id: "R1", text: "After a deploy by `infra.apply` of a Worker that has a version before it, the deployer shall put that Worker on probation: state `probation`, with the hash, the hash before it and the time. Today this is so for the kernel, the core and the page's Worker only. `memberDeploy` is untouched.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_an_applied_worker_is_on_probation_after_a_deploy" },
  { id: "R2", text: "While a Worker is on probation, when 10 minutes (`probationMs`) have passed with no passing verdict, the deployer shall put it back to the version before with the reason `not confirmed in 10 minutes`, as `nextKernelState` words it today.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_unverified_worker_is_put_back_at_the_limit" },
  { id: "R3", text: "While a Worker is on probation, an apply, a redistil with apply, or a remove of that Worker shall be refused with the string under Words, and a deploy of any other Worker in the same call shall go on.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_refuses_a_deploy_over_an_unverified_one" },
  { id: "R4", text: "After a deploy, and before it answers the apply, the deployer shall buy browser time (`browser.extend`, 120 s) and make one run (`browser.run`) of the Brain's page with that Worker's module alone in view. The apply's result for the Worker is then `verified`, `put-back`, or `probation` when the run gave no verdict.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_starts_one_verify_run_per_deploy; on cb4: logs.query at=deploy.verify after an apply" },
  { id: "R5", text: "The verdict shall carry the hash the page loaded. The deployer shall act on it only when that hash is the one it deployed and is still running; any other verdict is logged and dropped.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_drops_a_verdict_for_another_hash" },
  { id: "R6", text: "When the verdict is a pass, the deployer shall end the probation, record the hash as confirmed, and tell the core, which marks the route verified.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_pass_ends_probation; on cb4: service.list shows verified.ok for the hash" },
  { id: "R7", text: "When the verdict is a fail (a test threw, or had no result in 30 s), the deployer shall put the Worker back at once with the failed tests in the reason.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_fail_puts_back_at_once" },
  { id: "R8", text: "When the run gives no verdict (the browser service is down, the page did not load, the run passed its time), the deployer shall log `deploy.verify.none` and change nothing. R2 then decides.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_no_verdict_changes_nothing" },
  { id: "R9", text: "The Brain's page shall load a Worker's module only when that module is in the view or is opened by a click. With one module in view, no other Worker's module is loaded and none of its `test_` cells run. The verdict says how many other tests ran (`otherTestsRan`).", check: "cloud-brain: test_page_loads_only_the_modules_in_view; on cb4: otherTestsRan of deploy.verified is the count of the shell's own tests and no service's" },
  { id: "R10", text: "The Brain's page shall send no verdict. `verifyService` runs tests for the reader and reports nothing; `deploy.report` on the core answers only the deployer.", check: "cloud-brain: test_page_sends_no_report; brain-core: test_core_deploy_report_is_the_deployers" },
  { id: "R11", text: "For `brain-x-issues` on cb4 the verdict shall arrive within 60 s of the deploy.", check: "on cb4: the `at` of deploy.verified minus the `at` of the deploy, from logs.query. Measured by hand 2026-10-10 22:35: 9 s from opening the tab to the verdict." },
  { id: "R13", text: "A module with no `test_` cell is verified: the verdict is a pass with `tests: 0`, and the log line says so.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_a_module_with_no_tests_is_verified" },
  { id: "R12", text: "A Worker deployed for the first time has nothing to be put back to. Its run is made, the verdict is recorded (`verified` or `failing`), and it is not on probation.", check: "brain-deployer: test_deployer_first_deploy_is_verified_with_nothing_to_put_back" }
]
// %%
requirementsTable = htl.html`<table style="font-size:.9em">
  <thead><tr><th>id</th><th>requirement</th><th>check</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${requirements.map((r) => htl.html`<tr data-requirement=${r.id}><td><strong>${r.id}</strong></td><td>${r.text}</td><td>${r.check ? htl.html`<code>${r.check}</code>` : htl.html`<em>none</em>`}</td></tr>`)}</tbody>
</table>`
// %%
md`### Worked examples

What the verify page left on \`window.__verify\`, read with \`browser.eval\` on cb4 at 22:35 CEST on 2026-10-10 (\`tools/scratch/cloud-brain-experiments/verify-page/\`). \`worker\`, \`module\` and \`hash\` are what R5 reads.`
// %%
examples = [
  { given: "brain-x-issues 15d722ba45e4, its module alone, Chrome 128 on cb4", expect: { worker: "brain-x-issues", module: "@tomlarkworthy/brain-issues", hash: "15d722ba45e4bce31309478f7e49d36427f199dccd97c41383c7e326c597a6ff", ok: true, tests: 19, failed: [], bootMs: 1086, testMs: 3784, otherTestsRan: 0, heapMB: 93 } },
  { given: "the same module in the whole published notebook, same browser, 22:11", expect: { tests: 19, noResultIn60s: 18, heapMB: 640 } },
  { given: "a verdict whose hash is not the one deployed", expect: "logged, dropped, the Worker stays on probation" }
]
