md`## Design

The parts and the order. The deployer is the only actor that decides. The browser is a place the tests run, and holds no credential.`
// %%
architecture = onPaper(mermaid`flowchart LR
  apply[infra.apply] --> dep[deployer]
  dep -- deploys --> w[Worker NAME]
  dep -- run the verify page for NAME --> br[brain-x-browser]
  br --> page[the Brain page, NAME module alone in view]
  page -. getSource, public .-> w
  page -- verdict with hash --> dep
  dep -- pass: confirm, fail: put back --> w
  dep -- verified or failing --> core[brain-core route]
  tick[one-minute tick] -- 10 minutes and no pass: put back --> dep`)
// %%
sequence = onPaper(mermaid`sequenceDiagram
  participant C as brain.ts apply
  participant D as deployer
  participant W as Worker NAME
  participant B as brain-x-browser
  C->>D: infra.apply NAME (was HASH0)
  D->>W: deploy HASH1
  D->>D: probation NAME, lastGood HASH0
  D->>B: extend 120 s, run the page with NAME in view
  B->>W: getSource NAME
  B-->>D: verdict hash HASH1, ok, failed
  alt pass and HASH1 is running
    D->>D: confirmed
  else fail and HASH1 is running
    D->>W: put back HASH0
  else no verdict
    D->>D: nothing, at 10 minutes put back HASH0
  end
  D-->>C: verified, put-back or probation
  C->>D: infra.apply NAME again, while on probation
  D-->>C: refused, not verified yet`)
// %%
md`### The verify page

It is the Brain's own page. Tom, 2026-10-10: "I thought the brain already served a shell and pulled in modules." It does: the page the kernel serves holds no service's module, and \`loadService\` (\`cloud-brain.ojs:766\`) reads a Worker's module with \`getSource\` and applies it to the page. What makes that page unfit to verify one Worker today:

- \`assembled\` loads the module of every Worker when the page opens (22 on cb4).
- The page boots 20 mains, \`@tomlarkworthy/tests\` among them, which runs every \`test_\` cell that is present.

Tom, 2026-10-10: "I think the brain shoudl default to loading only modules in its view or that are clicked". So:

- The page loads a Worker's module when it is in the view (\`#view=…\`) or when its link is clicked, and not before. The Services table is drawn from the registry (\`service.list\`) without the modules.
- The deployer's run is \`browser.run\` on \`<brain>/#view=C100(S100(MODULE))\` with no session, a \`wait\` that is true when the verdict is there, and an \`expression\` that reads it. The verdict is made by a cell of the page's module that runs the \`test_\` cells of the module in view, 30 s each, and checks \`getInfo\` gives the hash that was loaded (as \`verifyService\` does today).
- The page sends nothing. The run's answer goes back to the deployer, which called it.

Not measured: this page with one module in view, in the cluster's browser. The shell's own 20 mains still boot and their tests run beside the module's. Step 1 measures it. What was measured is the module with only its imports booted, in a file made by a probe: 19 of 19 in 3.8 s, heap 93 MB (\`tools/scratch/cloud-brain-experiments/verify-page/\`). The probe's file-making is not part of this design.

### How the deployer waits

The run is awaited inside the apply, after the Worker is deployed and put on probation, and the apply answers with the verdict. \`browser.run\` ends at 120 s at most. The deployer has no \`ctx.waitUntil\` of its own today (\`cloudflare-iac.ojs:692\` is the wrapper's), and a run started from the one-minute tick could use all 60 s of R11 before it began. If the request ends before the run does (the client went away), the Worker stays on probation and R2 decides. \`brain-x-browser\` takes a call from the deployer: its rule is \`caller.trusted || caller.kind in ["worker", "deployer"]\` (\`brain-browser.ojs:125\`). Every method but \`extend\`, \`list\` and \`status\` answers 409 \`NoTime\` with no bought time (\`brain-browser.ojs:48\`), so the deployer buys before it runs.

### Decisions

| decided | instead of | because |
|---|---|---|
| The deployer starts the run and decides | the owner's page reporting (today) | the page's verdict depended on the browser and on what else the page ran; 2026-10-10 19:32 |
| Tests run in a browser of the Brain | bun with no browser (\`cloud-brain-dev/test.ts\`) | at 22:33 that runner had 7 failures not yet explained and 28 tests that need a DOM (its author's count). Tom: "it has not got the tests working though. I think maybe browser route is better" |
| Browser Run as it is, Chrome 128 | a container with a current Chrome | no image to keep; 19 of 19 in 3.8 s. The cost is the non-goal above |
| The Brain's page loads only the modules in view or clicked | loading every Worker's module on opening (today); a separate verify file made by the deployer or a tool | Tom: "I think the brain shoudl default to loading only modules in its view or that are clicked". The published notebook with everything booted: 18 of 19 had no result in 60 s |
| The run is awaited inside the apply | a run started by the tick | the tick is a minute apart; Tom: "its mega important the verification occur quickly" |
| A module with no tests is verified | putting it back at 10 minutes | there is nothing to fail; the health check still applies |
| Probation for every Worker, 10 minutes | health check only for recipes (today) | Tom: "the 10 min rollback is mandatory liveliness feature" |
| An unverified Worker blocks its own next deploy only | blocking every deploy | Tom: "yes block jsut that one worker that is not verified, other deploys go on." |
| The tracker signs with ECDSA P-256 | keeping Ed25519 with a second code path | done 2026-10-10 22:04, \`tools/cloud-brain/records/sign-p256.md\` |

### Known traps

- \`brain.ts confirm\` with no name ends the probation of every Worker at once (\`brain-deployer.ojs:823\`, \`confirm = async (only = null)\`; \`brain.ts:269\` sends \`{}\`). After R1 that would confirm every unverified recipe with no test. See the open questions.
- A service's module is not in the Brain's page. The page loads it from the Worker.
- \`browser.open\` refuses a URL that is not http or https, so a blank tab cannot be asked for.
- \`browser.run\` ends at 120 s at most. A module whose tests need longer cannot be verified by one run.
- The page's \`verifyService\` also loads a Worker's module into the reader's page (\`loadService\`). That stays. Only the report goes.`
// %%
md`## Handover

This changes the deployer, the core and the page. \`rules.md\` of the brain-maintenance loop names the kernel, \`brain-core\`, \`brain-db\` and the deployer as the owner's to deploy; the page's Worker is a recipe that goes on probation. Write, test and stop; Tom deploys all three.

### Where the work goes

- \`tools/cloud-brain/brain-deployer.ojs\`: \`nextKernelState\` (line 41) already has the three transitions; R1 is the condition at line 525 that puts only non-recipes on probation. R3 is in the \`change\` callbacks that \`apply\` (line 610) gives \`guard\` for a deploy and for a remove, and in \`redistil\` with apply. Not inside \`guard\` itself: the roll back button of the deployer page, \`memberDeploy\` and \`memberRemove\` also go through \`guard\` and are not refused. R4 to R8: a function beside \`verdict\` (line 849) that buys the browser time and makes the run after \`rows.put("worker/…")\`, awaited inside the apply, and \`verdictOf\` (line 850), which already drops a stale hash (it logs nothing today) and puts back on a fail. The tick (line 796) already puts back at the limit for what is on probation, with the reason \`not confirmed in 10 minutes\`, which is kept. \`memberDeploy\` (line 966) is a separate path and is not changed.
- \`tools/cloud-brain/brain-core.ojs\`: \`deploy.report\` (line 524) and \`toDeployer\` (line 422). The core keeps \`verified\` on the route; it is told by the deployer.
- \`tools/cloud-brain/cloud-brain.ojs\`: \`verifyService\` (line 790), \`assembled\` (line 826), \`assembledRefresh\`, \`loadService\` (line 766).
- \`tools/cloud-brain/cloud-brain-docs.ojs\` line 240 and \`cloud-brain-specs.ojs\` R9, which describe the page reporting.
- Read first: \`knowledge/working-with-cloud-brain-remote-lopecode-cluster.md\`, \`.claude/skills/brain-maintenance/philosophy.md\` (Clarifications).

Line numbers are of commit \`79e502e2\`.

### Boundaries

| | |
|---|---|
| **always** | seed, \`build.ts\`, the module's tests in a browser, then emit. Report the test output. |
| **ask first** | a change to \`cloudflare-iac.ojs\` (it moves every Worker's hash); a new method on \`brain-x-browser\`; a new Worker |
| **never** | deploy the kernel, the core, the database or the deployer; open the Brain's page in the cluster's browser before step 5 is on cb4; scan notebook HTML with a regular expression |

### Checks

\`\`\`
bun tools/cloud-brain/build.ts
bun tools/lope-tests.ts lopebooks/notebooks/@tomlarkworthy_cloud-brain.html --filter test_ --report tools/cloud-brain/.emitted/verify.ctrf.json
BRAIN_BASE=cb4 bun tools/cloud-brain/brain.ts redistil        # every line same
\`\`\`

\`--filter test_deployer\` matches 2 of the deployer's 42 tests and \`--filter test_core\` none of the core's 34, so the filter is every test. In a browser, run every \`test_\` cell of \`@tomlarkworthy/brain-deployer\`, \`@tomlarkworthy/brain-core\` and \`@tomlarkworthy/cloud-brain\` with the snippet of step 8 in \`.claude/skills/brain-maintenance/agents/implementer.md\`, and report the three counts.

Existing tests this change rewrites, by name: \`test_a_deploy_is_unverified_until_its_tests_are_reported\` (\`brain-core.ojs:1297\`) and \`test_a_failed_verdict_puts_the_deploy_back\` (\`brain-deployer.ojs:1918\`), which test the page's report. \`test_probation_transitions\` (\`:1508\`) and \`test_kernel_probation\` (\`:1974\`) assert \`not confirmed in\`, which is kept; they must still pass.

After Tom's deploys, on cb4: apply a recipe twice within a minute (the second answers \`refused\` with the string under Words), and read \`logs.query\` for \`deploy.verify\` and \`deploy.verified\` of the first.

### Steps

1. Page: load only the modules in view or clicked (R9), and the cell that makes the verdict. Tom deploys the page's Worker. Then by hand, \`browser.run\` on cb4 for three Workers: \`brain-x-issues\`, \`brain-x-browser\`, \`brain-x-static\`. **Stop and show** the three verdicts, times, heap and \`otherTestsRan\`. If the shell's own tests make a run slow, that is a finding for Tom and not a reason to change the design alone.
2. Deployer: R1, R2, R3 with their tests. No run yet, so every deploy is put back at 10 minutes unless confirmed by hand. Do not deploy this step alone.
3. Deployer: R4 to R8 and R12 with their tests, the browser stood in for by a fake in the rig. **Stop and show** the tests.
4. Core: \`deploy.report\` for the deployer only. Page: R10.
5. Tom deploys the deployer, the core and the page to cb4. Then the checks above on cb4. **Stop and show.**
6. Docs and the as-built record. Then the owner closes \`deployer-put-back-issues-worker\` and \`verify-in-hidden-tab-fails-tests\`, and removes the line of \`rules.md\` about the cluster's browser.`
// %%
md`## Open questions

A question marked blocking stops the handover. Each open one is listed in the strip at the top with a link to its row here. Rows with an answer are Tom's, with his words.`
// %%
questions = [
  { q: "Where is the verify page made?", blocking: false, answer: "It is the Brain's page with one module in view. Tom, 2026-10-10: \"I thought the brain already served a shell and pulled in modules.\" and \"I think the brain shoudl default to loading only modules in its view or that are clicked\"." },
  { q: "The Brain's page boots @tomlarkworthy/tests, which runs every test_ cell present. With one module in view that is the shell's own tests and the module's. Does the tests module stay a main of the Brain's page? Claude would keep it and measure in step 1; if the shell's tests slow a run, drop it from the mains and let the Tests pane load it when opened.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "A deploy of brain-x-browser, of the page's Worker or of the core can break the thing that verifies it. Is the 10 minute limit enough for those, with no other check? Claude would say yes: a broken verifier gives no verdict, and R2 puts the Worker back.", blocking: true, answer: "" },
  { q: "The deployer buys 120 s of browser time for each run: 0.003 USD at the rate in brain-browser (N x 0.000025), charged to the caller `deployer`. brain-x-browser takes the deployer's call (brain-browser.ojs:125). Is that spend accepted with no daily limit, or is a limit wanted? With a limit reached, runs give no verdict and every deploy is put back at 10 minutes.", blocking: true, answer: "" },
  { q: "brain.ts confirm with no name confirms every Worker on probation (brain-deployer.ojs:823). After this change that would keep every unverified recipe with no test run. Claude would make a hand confirm name its Worker, and leave it to the owner's session and the recovery key.", blocking: true, answer: "" },
  { q: "Is a module with no test_ cell verified, failed, or refused at deploy? Claude wrote R13 as verified. Every brain-*.ojs has at least one test today.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "May a deploy signed with the recovery key go over an unverified Worker? It is the way out when a verifier is broken and the fix is to a Worker on probation. Claude would allow it and log it.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "After a put back for failed tests, is the same hash refused if it is sent again? Today nothing remembers a failed hash. Claude would not add it: the run is the cost of sending it again.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "The deployer and the kernel are deployed by install-deployer and by the recovery path, not by infra.apply. Are they verified by this run too, or left to their probation and brain.ts confirm as today? Claude would leave them as today in this change.", blocking: false, answer: "" },
  { q: "Does a report have to arrive for a deploy to stand?", blocking: false, answer: "No. Tom, 2026-10-10: \"It should not be mandatory because it might be lost.\" With no report the 10 minute limit decides (R2, R8)." },
  { q: "Is every deploy blocked while one Worker is unverified?", blocking: false, answer: "No. Tom, 2026-10-10: \"yes block jsut that one worker that is not verified, other deploys go on.\"" },
  { q: "If the browser service is down, every deploy is put back at 10 minutes. Is that accepted?", blocking: false, answer: "Yes. Tom, 2026-10-10: \"the 10 min rollback is mandatory liveliness feature. We can't have the next thing building on a faulty base.\"" },
  { q: "Browser, or tests with no browser?", blocking: false, answer: "Browser. Tom, 2026-10-10: \"I think maybe browser route is better, but the repository work is still very useful\"." }
]
// %%
questionsTable = htl.html`<table style="font-size:.9em">
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>question</th><th>answer</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>${questions.map((q, i) => htl.html`<tr data-question=${i}><td>${q.blocking && !q.answer ? "blocking" : ""}</td><td>${q.q}</td><td>${q.answer || htl.html`<em>open</em>`}</td></tr>`)}</tbody>
</table>`
// %%
states = []
// %%
// mermaid draws dark text on no background, which is unreadable on a dark theme.
onPaper = (node) => htl.html`<div style="max-width:640px;background:#f6f7f9;border-radius:6px;padding:10px">${node}</div>`
// %%
specModule = "__SPEC_MODULE__"
// %%
import {md} from "@tomlarkworthy/editable-md"
// %%
import {annotation, annotations} from "@tomlarkworthy/annotate"
// %%
import {sticky} from "@tomlarkworthy/sticky"

